1 OCCU Borgermester i Trondheim 1540 - 1594, Borgermester i Nidaros, Borgermester/ i Nidaros, Falkefanger, handelsmann og senere rådmann og borgermester i Trondheim, Falkoneer og ordfører i Trondheim, Borgermester i Trondhjem 1540-1594, 
2 CONC Borgermester, Tr.heim
1 TITL Skipsreder
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Adrian Rochertsen Falkener''' (c. 1500 – d. before 1597) was a legendary Dutch- or North German-born falconer, shipowner, and long-serving burgomaster (mayor) of Trondheim. He became the progenitor of the promin
2 CONC ent Norwegian Falch/Falck family.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Personal Life ===
2 CONT His origins are enveloped in legend, but his name strongly suggests he was of Dutch or North German descent. In historical records, his patronymic is written in various ways, including Rotkersson, Rutgersøn, and Rochertssen. Accor
2 CONC ding to older family traditions and lineage books, he supposedly lived to the exceptional age of 120 years and fathered 24 children (18 sons and 6 daughters)—claims that modern historians dismiss as pure fiction or heavy exaggerat
2 CONC ions. He was likely born around 1500 and passed away sometime between the autumn of 1592 and January 1597. His widow is recorded as being alive on January 12, 1597.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Career ===
2 CONT * '''Falconer:''' His surname/byname indicates that he began his career as a falconer, likely in service to the King. A record from the Norwegian Fief Accounts (Norske Lensregnskapsbøker) from 1548 shows that an "Adriann falchener
2 CONC " delivered falcons under the Steinvikholm fief, confirming his occupation.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster of Trondheim:''' He may have resided in Bergen early on before establishing himself in Trondheim around the 1520s. On July 5, 1547, he is mentioned for the first time in official records as a burgomaster of Trondh
2 CONC eim, when he sealed a property transfer at Østråt. In early documents, he is always listed after the primary burgomaster, Niels Lauritsen, but he later advanced to become the senior burgomaster.
2 CONT * '''Tenure:''' He is regularly documented as an active burgomaster throughout the 1500s, including in 1562, 1578, 1581, and 1589. On May 1, 1591, he and the secondary burgomaster, Peder Ericksen, sent a letter to the King request
2 CONC ing to be excused from personally attending the royal oath-taking ceremony at Akershus Castle, describing themselves as "two poor, old, and frail men." The final mention of him in office is dated October 31, 1592. By November 21, 
2 CONC 1596, Petter Nielsen had assumed his position.
2 CONT
2 CONT === The Northern Seven Years' War and Pro-Swedish Stance ===
2 CONT During the Northern Seven Years' War (1563–1570), Adrian Falkener distinguished himself as an ardent supporter of the Swedish forces. When Swedish troops under the command of Claude Collart occupied Trondheim in 1564, they were we
2 CONC lcomed with open arms. Falkener served as Collart's advisor, and it is recorded that he sent wine as a gift to the Swedish commander during the siege of Steinvikholm Fortress. In gratitude for his support, he received valuable ass
2 CONC ets and properties that the Swedes had confiscated from wealthy anti-Swedish citizens in Trøndelag.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Wealth and Properties ===
2 CONT Falkener was one of Trondheim's wealthiest individuals. In 1563, he is registered as a sea captain, and by 1567, he was the city's largest shipowner, operating two "jekter" (cargo boats) in the trade routes to Finnmark. He owned s
2 CONC everal prominent properties:
2 CONT * '''Falkenergården:''' His urban estate down by Brattøra (corresponding to the modern address Olav Tryggvasons gate 5). He also constructed a new house adjacent to the old one here.
2 CONT * '''Adriansløkken:''' A vast plot of land and property just outside the city limits in Ila (later known as Arildsløkken).
2 CONT * '''Rural Estates:''' He owned shares and parcels in the farms Haugan at Byneset and Kvåle in Orkdal.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and Descendants ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Adrian Falkener is recorded as having been married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1st Marriage:''' Married to '''NN Corneliusdatter'' Lights''. She had previously been married to a man named Paul, with whom she had two sons, Henrik and Cornelius Paulsen (both residing in Bergen).
2 CONT * ''Children from the 1st marriage (among others):''
2 CONT ** '''Rochert (Rickert) Adriansen:''' Born c. 1542. He matriculated as a student in Rostock in 1562 and later served as the parish priest at Ørlandet.
2 CONT ** '''Peter (Peder) Adriansen Falkner:''' Born c. 1538, died June 29, 1569, in Bergen. He was a merchant in Trondheim but was fatally stabbed in the head at Bergenhus Fortress by the lagman (lawspeaker) of Stavanger, Christopher N
2 CONC ielssøn Grøn, following a heated argument regarding the reliability of the Dutch. He died five days later (many attributed his death to poor medical treatment). Peder was married to Gunnhild Sevaldsdatter/Sigurdsdatter (daughter o
2 CONC f Sir Sigurd Amundsen of Verdal). Their children were:
2 CONT *** '''Rochert Pedersen:''' Became an alderman (rådmann) and burgher in Trondheim.
2 CONT *** '''Margrethe Pedersdatter Falkner:''' (c. 1556–1622), married Jacob Pedersen, bailiff (fogd) of the Giske estate and later lagman in Trondheim. (Through their son, the bailiff Peter Jacobsen Falch of Helgeland, comes a vast li
2 CONC neage of descendants, including the famous poet-priest Petter Dass).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2nd Marriage:''' Married before 1570 to '''Margrethe Pedersdatter''' (died after 1597), who survived him. She was also a widow and had a son, Nils Olsen (a clerk in Fosnes), from her first marriage.
2 CONT * ''Other known children (maternal placement varies across some sources):''
2 CONT ** '''Anna Adriansdatter:''' Married 1) churchwarden Giert Borgersen in Bergen, and 2) town bailiff (byfogd) Claus Miltzow in Bergen.
2 CONT ** '''Cornelius Adriansen:''' Served as captain on his mother's ship, died before 1597 in Trondheim.
2 CONT ** '''Michel Adriansen:''' Acquired burgher status (borgerskap) in Bergen in 1574.
2 CONT ** '''Jens Adriansen:''' Died of the plague in Bergen on October 4, 1567.
2 CONT
