1 OCCU Borgermester i Trondheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Niels Jensen Skriver''' (died before 1610) was a prominent alderman (rådmann), merchant, and burgomaster (mayor) of Trondheim around the turn of the 17th century. He was one of the largest civilian landowners in
2 CONC  Trøndelag during his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Personal Life ===
2 CONT By 1595, Niels Jensen was an elderly, well-established man, as records from that year state he had already been trading in Lappland for 30 years. He was still alive in 1604, and is documented in life as late as July 2, 1607, when 
2 CONC he issued a receipt in Trondheim. He passed away before 1610, when his wife is recorded as a widow in the fief accounts (lensregnskapet).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Career ===
2 CONT * '''Scribe and Alderman:''' His byname "Skriver" (Scribe) indicates a background as a professional scribe or clerk. It is possible he is the same "Nils Skriver" who was detained by Swedish forces alongside the lagman (lawspeaker)
2 CONC  in 1564 during the Northern Seven Years' War. He can be verified with certainty in primary sources from August 1, 1582, when he is styled as an alderman in Trondheim. He appears frequently as an alderman throughout the 1580s and 
2 CONC 1590s; notably when he acquired the Snertingen estate in Inderøy (July 5, 1588), when he attended the royal oath-taking ceremony at Akershus Castle (May 1, 1591), and when he served as Trondheim's proxy in a maritime boundary disp
2 CONC ute with Bergen (May 22, 1592).
2 CONT * '''Border Survey Against Russia:''' In 1595, King Christian IV specifically requested that Niels Jensen be dispatched northwards to participate in a border demarcation survey against Russia, reflecting his extensive knowledge of
2 CONC  the northern territories.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster:''' Niels Jensen Skriver was appointed burgomaster of Trondheim in 1599, appearing in office for the first time on July 5, 1599. He is further documented as active burgomaster in 1602, 1603, 1604, and finally in t
2 CONC he noted letter/receipt dated July 2, 1607, to the then-bailiff of Jämtland, Peder Grim, which opens with: ''"...Bekindes ieg Niels Jensen Borgemester Udi Trundhiemb..."'' (Acknowledged by I, Niels Jensen, Burgomaster in Trondheim
2 CONC ). He likely held the office until his death. (His omission from certain older lists online, such as Strinda Historielag, is due to incomplete transcriptions in older historical works).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Wealth and Properties ===
2 CONT Niels Jensen conducted extensive commercial operations. In 1595, it was reported that he had traded in Lappland for three decades, and by 1597, he owned his own merchant ship, which sailed routes to Lübeck (Lybek).
2 CONT
2 CONT He acquired—largely through his marriage—a vast portfolio of land and estates in Innherred and Namdalen, making him one of the wealthiest landowning burghers in Trøndelag at the time. When his widow Gunnhild is recorded in the fie
2 CONC f accounts in 1610, she was listed as the largest landowner in the Inderøy bailiwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Niels Jensen Skriver was married to '''Gunnhild Sevaldsdatter''' (also written Sevaldsdotter/Sevoldsdtr). She was the daughter of Sevald (and is erroneously cited by some older sources as a daughter of Sir Sigurd Amundsen of Verda
2 CONC l, a claim disproven by her patronymic and property inheritance records). Gunnhild was a first cousin of the Trondheim burgher Sevold Olsen, and she was the widow of the merchant Peder Adriansen Falch (son of burgomaster Adrian Ro
2 CONC chertsen Falkener), who was killed in Bergen in 1569.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT * '''Kirsten Nilsdatter (Kirstine Nielsdtr):''' Married the lagman of Jämtland and Trondheim, '''Peder Grim'''. This connection explains why Niels Jensen issued a receipt to Peder Grim (his future son-in-law) in 1607, and why Niel
2 CONC s Skriver is noted in parentheses alongside Kirstine in older genealogical registries (such as *Slægtebog over familien Borberg*, p. 137).
2 CONT * '''Peder Nielsen:''' Possibly the Peder Nilsen who owned shares in the Snertingen and Leira estates in 1617, and who may have been Niels' son.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Stepchildren (Gunnhild's children from her first marriage to Peder Falch):'''
2 CONT * '''Rochert Pettersen Falch:''' Became an alderman and town bailiff (byfogd) in Trondheim.
2 CONT * '''Margrete Pedersdatter Falch:''' Married lagman Jacob Pedersen (parents of Peter Jacobsen Falch of Helgeland, making them the grandparents of the famous poet-priest Petter Dass).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', p. 25 (section "Niels Jensen Skriver"). Citing the Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR Vol. III) and Court Records of the Norwegian Diet (NHD IV, V, VI). Refere
2 CONC nced from image {FA7E37D6-C4DC-43D7-BDBC-DEF7B7D69494}.jpg.
2 CONT * *Norsk Slektshistorisk Tidsskrift* (2002), Vol. 38, No. 3 (regarding the receipt of July 2, 1607, and the relation to Peder Grim).
2 CONT * Borberg, Laurits Ploug (1944): *Slægtebog over Familien Borberg*, p. 137 (regarding the lineage connection to Peder Grim and his daughter Kirstine).
2 CONT * Genealogical databases: vestraat.net, nermo.org, and zinow.no.
